Great context. So the core issue is:

New Relic fires alerts → Splunk On-Call opens incidents → maintenance ends → those incidents just sit there open, nobody closed them → chaos

And yes, New Relic alerts likely stay in a firing state until New Relic itself sees the metric recover and sends a RECOVERY signal. If the service came back healthy but New Relic didn't send that recovery (or Splunk On-Call missed it), the incident stays open forever.

