Secrets to store in Harness first
Go to Harness → Account/Org/Project Settings → Secrets and store:

Secret Name	Value
splunk_oncall_api_id	Your API ID (from Splunk On-Call → Integrations → API)
splunk_oncall_api_key	Your API Key
splunk_oncall_username	A valid service/bot user in your org (used for resolve calls)
Pipeline Structure
PRE-DEPLOY STAGE
└── Step: HTTP - Start Maintenance Mode
    └── Capture: maintenanceInstanceId (output variable)

DEPLOY STAGE
└── ... your existing steps ...

POST-DEPLOY STAGE
├── Step 1: HTTP - Get Open Incidents
│   └── Capture: list of triggered incident numbers
├── Step 2: HTTP - Resolve Open Incidents  ← BEFORE ending maintenance
└── Step 3: HTTP - End Maintenance Mode
Step 1: Start Maintenance Mode (Pre-Deploy)
HTTP Step config:

Method:  POST
URL:     https://api.victorops.com/api-public/v1/maintenancemode/start
Headers:
  X-VO-Api-Id:   <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_id")>
  X-VO-Api-Key:  <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_key")>
  Content-Type:  application/json

Body:
{
  "purpose": "Harness Deploy - <+pipeline.name> - <+pipeline.executionId>",
  "type": "RoutingKeys",
  "names": ["your-routing-key-here"]   // or omit 'names' + use "type":"Global" for all
}
Output Variable — capture the maintenance ID:

In the HTTP step's Output tab, add:

Variable Name:  maintenanceModeId
Value:          <+json("activeInstances[0].instanceId")>
This maintenanceModeId flows into your post-deploy steps.

Step 2: Get Open Incidents (Post-Deploy, Step 1)
Method:  GET
URL:     https://api.victorops.com/api-public/v1/incidents
Headers:
  X-VO-Api-Id:   <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_id")>
  X-VO-Api-Key:  <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_key")>
Output Variable:

Variable Name:  openIncidentNumbers
Value:          <+json("incidents[*].incidentNumber")>
This gives you an array of all currently open/triggered incident numbers.

Step 3: Resolve Open Incidents (Post-Deploy, Step 2)
This is the critical step — runs before ending maintenance mode.

Method:  PATCH
URL:     https://api.victorops.com/api-public/v1/incidents/resolve
Headers:
  X-VO-Api-Id:   <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_id")>
  X-VO-Api-Key:  <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_key")>
  Content-Type:  application/json

Body:
{
  "userName": "<+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_username")>",
  "incidentNames": <+stage.spec.execution.steps.GetOpenIncidents.output.outputVariables.openIncidentNumbers>,
  "message": "Auto-resolved by Harness post-deploy - <+pipeline.executionId>"
}
Step 4: End Maintenance Mode (Post-Deploy, Step 3)
Method:  PUT
URL:     https://api.victorops.com/api-public/v1/maintenancemode/<+stage.spec.execution.steps.StartMaintenanceMode.output.outputVariables.maintenanceModeId>/end
Headers:
  X-VO-Api-Id:   <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_id")>
  X-VO-Api-Key:  <+secrets.getValue("splunk_oncall_api_key")>
No body needed — the PUT to the end endpoint is sufficient.

Important: Handle Pipeline Failures
You need these post-deploy steps to run even if the deploy fails, otherwise you'll be stuck in maintenance mode forever. In Harness, on each post-deploy step set:

Run condition: Always
Or wrap the post-deploy steps in a Failure Strategy that runs the cleanup stage regardless of prior step status.

One Gotcha to Watch For
The GET incidents endpoint returns all open incidents org-wide, not just ones from your maintenance window. If other teams have unrelated open incidents, this will resolve those too.

To scope it tighter, use the reporting API instead for Step 2, which lets you filter by startedAfter timestamp:

GET https://api.victorops.com/api-reporting/v1/incidents
  ?startedAfter=<+pipeline.startTs>
  &currentPhase=triggered
This only grabs incidents that opened after your pipeline started — much safer in a shared org. Worth doing if multiple teams share the same Splunk On-Call instance.

